## The Corner Shield

The emerald cut has four corners. Unlike a round brilliant, which has no corners and no acute angles, the emerald cut's step-cut table terminates at 90-degree right angles at each of its four ends. These corners are the most mechanically vulnerable points on the stone.

A chip on a round brilliant requires significant force and a direct hit on the culet or girdle. A chip on an emerald cut corner can happen from a moderately hard impact on a hard surface — a kitchen counter, a steering wheel, a doorframe.

The right angle concentrates impact stress into a small area. The crystal structure of diamond at a right-angle corner is under more stress than at a curved surface.

This is why the emerald cut is almost always set with corner prongs — prongs positioned specifically at the stone's four corners, not along its long sides. Corner prongs protect the most vulnerable points. A setting without corner protection leaves the emerald cut's edges exposed.

For reference, the most protected prong configuration for an emerald cut is a six-prong setting with prongs at each corner and one along each long side. Even this format leaves the stone's corner angles slightly exposed between prong tips. The bezel has no such gap — every corner is fully enclosed at every point.

The bezel solves this completely. The continuous metal band wraps each corner with full metal contact. There are no exposed angles.

[![Bezel set emerald cut diamond ring in 14K yellow gold, showing the full corner protection of the continuous metal bezel surrounding the rectangular step-cut stone](/images/bezel-set-emerald-cut-diamond-ring-yellow-gold.avif)](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings?styleIDs=bezel&ShapeID=Emerald&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational)

The entire perimeter of the stone, including all four corners, is enclosed within the metal collar. Impact force distributes across the metal before reaching the stone. This is The Corner Shield: the bezel is the only solitaire setting format that fully protects every corner of the emerald cut.

For buyers who regularly work outdoors, exercise with their ring on, have active jobs, or travel frequently, this is not a minor consideration. A chipped corner on a 1ct emerald cut affects both the stone's appearance and its resale value permanently. The bezel eliminates this specific failure mode.

The EC Side Stone designs extend this protection further — the bezel-set side stones flank the center stone and provide additional metal mass around the setting. At [$4,300 in yellow gold](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/bezel-set-emerald-cut-side-stone-diamond-engagement-ring-in-14k-yellow-gold-by-james-allen-item-304633?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational), this is the most complete corner protection build in the dataset for an emerald cut stone.

